00:10:26,780 --> 00:10:28,300
Paying out the maximum jackpot

160
00:10:28,301 --> 00:10:30,460
could take more than three days.

161
00:10:31,360 --> 00:10:33,200
Thankfully, this game takes tickets.

162
00:10:34,020 --> 00:10:35,260
In the old days, we'd have coins,

163
00:10:35,520 --> 00:10:37,780
but the games would either run out of coins,

164
00:10:38,020 --> 00:10:41,060
so we'd have to stop the player and refill the game,

165
00:10:41,140 --> 00:10:43,840
and that takes time and inconveniences the guest.

166
00:10:43,841 --> 00:10:47,440
or if it's a big win, the game may not have enough coins in it

167
00:10:47,441 --> 00:10:49,040
and we couldn't actually pay the customer.

168
00:10:49,760 --> 00:10:52,500
Phasing out coins has changed the casino experience,

169
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:56,380
but it hasn't changed a few persistent superstitions

170
00:10:56,381 --> 00:11:00,500
held by fans of coin-operated and modern slots alike.

171
00:11:01,160 --> 00:11:03,980
In casinos, often customers have a perception that

172
00:11:03,981 --> 00:11:07,560
if they're playing a game and maybe they step up and leave that game

173
00:11:07,561 --> 00:11:09,460
and someone immediately sits down behind them

174
00:11:09,461 --> 00:11:11,740
and may play and hit a jackpot that,

175
00:11:11,741 --> 00:11:14,280
oh, if I just stayed there, that would have been my jackpot.

176
00:11:14,580 --> 00:11:15,720
Well, that's not always the case.

177
00:11:16,420 --> 00:11:18,480
In fact, that's almost never the case,

178
00:11:18,960 --> 00:11:20,920
thanks to a cutting-edge computer program

179
00:11:20,921 --> 00:11:24,240
embedded in both coin-operated and high-tech machines.

180
00:11:25,400 --> 00:11:28,140
It's called the Random Number Generator,

181
00:11:28,400 --> 00:11:31,440
and it's a program that cycles through random groups of numbers.

182
00:11:32,520 --> 00:11:34,720
The RNG is always cycling,

183
00:11:35,160 --> 00:11:37,100
even when the machine isn't in play.

184
00:11:38,260 --> 00:11:41,140
But once a player inserts money in places of bet,

185
00:11:41,740 --> 00:11:49,580
The instant a button is pushed or a lever is pulled, the random number generator picks the number combination highlighted in its system at that moment.

186
00:11:50,240 --> 00:11:53,260
Each reel stops in a position that corresponds to the number sequence.

187
00:11:54,540 --> 00:12:02,980
If the numbers and symbols on the pay line match a winning combination, the game processor calculates the amount won, and the machine pays out.

188
00:12:03,720 --> 00:12:06,620
With each new play, this random process repeats,

189
00:12:07,680 --> 00:12:11,860
ensuring that every play is entirely unrelated to a previous win or loss.

190
00:12:12,860 --> 00:12:16,200
In other words, there's no such thing as a hot machine.

191
00:12:17,080 --> 00:12:19,200
And there's no way to game the system.

192
00:12:22,460 --> 00:12:25,120
For slots fans, this may be a bit of a downer.

193
00:12:25,760 --> 00:12:28,860
But with some patience, most players will win something.

194
00:12:28,861 --> 00:12:35,860
That's because by law, slot machines have to pay out at least 80 cents for every dollar inserted,

195
00:12:35,861 --> 00:12:40,860
whether that dollar is a bill or four quarters.

196
00:12:40,861 --> 00:12:47,860
Despite the modernization of the slot machine, the old coin-operated versions have definite staying power.

197
00:12:49,860 --> 00:12:56,860
The coin-operated industry wouldn't operate very well without a fundamental necessity.

198
00:12:56,861 --> 00:13:03,400
coins. Billions of them are created and circulated every year and all of them

199
00:13:03,401 --> 00:13:10,300
begin life in a factory like this. The U.S. Mint in Philadelphia is a 20,000

200
00:13:10,301 --> 00:13:15,360
square foot money-making machine. Its mission? To provide the nation with

201
00:13:15,361 --> 00:13:21,540
enough coinage to conduct its trade and commerce. The Mint operates six facilities

202
00:13:21,541 --> 00:13:25,800
across the U.S., but this is its oldest and biggest operation.

203
00:13:26,880 --> 00:13:30,100
This is the largest coin factory in the world.

204
00:13:30,300 --> 00:13:34,940
In fact, every day here in Philadelphia, we produce about 20 million coins.

205
00:13:35,580 --> 00:13:38,420
The life of a coin begins with a congressional act

206
00:13:38,421 --> 00:13:41,620
that outlines specific requirements, like denomination,

207
00:13:42,200 --> 00:13:43,800
and who or what will be featured.

208
00:13:44,300 --> 00:13:50,500
Congress passes statute or law that directs the United States

209
00:13:50,501 --> 00:13:55,300
meant to make coinage, its appearance, its thickness, very specifically.

210
00:13:55,301 --> 00:13:59,068
The bill is then passed to an exclusive group of artists at the Mint.

211
00:13:59,069 --> 00:14:00,160
There are only seven

212
00:14:00,161 --> 00:14:01,633
of them in the U.S.

213
00:14:01,634 --> 00:14:07,060
and it's their job to turn Congress' requirements into actual coinage.

214
00:14:07,061 --> 00:14:10,160
It's one of the most unique jobs in the country.

215
00:14:10,161 --> 00:14:15,380
This is probably one of the only places in the world where the artwork that you generate

216
00:14:15,381 --> 00:14:17,800
It goes out to virtually everyone.

217
00:14:18,060 --> 00:14:18,960
Everyone touches it.

218
00:14:19,620 --> 00:14:22,820
So it's not only in the United States, it's all over the world.

219
00:14:23,400 --> 00:14:25,320
We find that humbling, you know,

220
00:14:25,380 --> 00:14:28,380
because very rarely an artist gets to that level.

221
00:14:29,100 --> 00:14:30,740
Currently, the Mint's big project

222
00:14:30,741 --> 00:14:34,740
is a series of presidential $1 coins for circulation.

223
00:14:35,880 --> 00:14:37,900
They feature deceased U.S. presidents

224
00:14:37,901 --> 00:14:39,280
in the order that they served.

225
00:14:41,200 --> 00:14:44,280
The 16th coin to be minted is our 16th president,

226
00:14:44,281 --> 00:14:45,880
Abraham Lincoln.

227
00:14:46,720 --> 00:14:50,680
The coin was designed and sculpted by veteran artist Don Everhart.

228
00:14:51,540 --> 00:14:53,480
The way it came about, which is kind of interesting,

229
00:14:53,720 --> 00:14:56,780
was the first design that I had actually done completely on the computer.

230
00:14:57,140 --> 00:14:58,860
I didn't use a pencil at all for this.

231
00:14:59,240 --> 00:15:01,840
I started to use a pencil, and I didn't like the way it was turning out,

232
00:15:01,900 --> 00:15:05,600
so I went to the computer and started working on the screen and drawing,

233
00:15:05,960 --> 00:15:08,940
and I really liked the way it looked, so it just kind of flowed.

234
00:15:09,080 --> 00:15:12,200
The portrait of Lincoln is based on a Civil War-era photograph

235
00:15:12,201 --> 00:15:13,860
by Alexander Gardner.

236
00:15:14,280 --> 00:15:19,680
There's an intensity about his eyes where he's looking right at you that I think I captured.

237
00:15:19,681 --> 00:15:23,180
But sketching the coin is just the first step.

238
00:15:23,181 --> 00:15:27,180
Next, he's sculpting it. And that's tricky business.

239
00:15:27,181 --> 00:15:33,180
The design has to be almost entirely flat, so the finished coin is as streamlined as possible.

240
00:15:33,181 --> 00:15:34,649
This is such low relief.

241
00:15:34,650 --> 00:15:39,180
Our maximum is 80 thousandths of an inch, and that's as high as we can go.

242
00:15:39,181 --> 00:15:41,180
We have to work within those parameters.

243
00:15:41,181 --> 00:15:45,060
80 thousandths of an inch is about as thin as a sheet of paper.

244
00:15:45,880 --> 00:15:49,720
Creating depth while working within that range is just a bit challenging.

245
00:15:50,360 --> 00:15:53,420
When you do a sculpture like this, it's really a lot of illusion.

246
00:15:53,920 --> 00:15:56,700
You have to do what I call painting with light,

247
00:15:56,860 --> 00:16:01,120
where you're sculpting planes and angles to catch light and to catch shadow

248
00:16:01,121 --> 00:16:04,620
so that you create a dynamic-looking portrait in this case.

249
00:16:05,440 --> 00:16:09,320
One of the final touches on any coin is a minuscule mark

250
00:16:09,321 --> 00:16:13,560
that goes unnoticed by most people, the artist's initials.

251
00:16:13,860 --> 00:16:16,340
They're hard to see in the actual coin, but you know they're there.

252
00:16:17,020 --> 00:16:19,660
If another artist designs it and I sculpt it,

253
00:16:19,740 --> 00:16:22,700
the other artist's initials would go on the left and mine would go on the right.

254
00:16:22,780 --> 00:16:26,480
In this case, I designed and sculpted it so my initials would go on the right side.

255
00:16:27,840 --> 00:16:31,160
Once it's finished, the model is handed over to the tooling team.

256
00:16:31,940 --> 00:16:33,980
They're in charge of making the hub and die,

257
00:16:34,740 --> 00:16:37,200
two crucial pieces necessary for coining.

258
00:16:37,201 --> 00:16:41,800
This steel cylinder will become what's called a reduction hub.

259
00:16:42,400 --> 00:16:46,160
It's essentially a coin-sized metal replica of the artist's model.

260
00:16:48,760 --> 00:16:52,360
The reduction hub will be mechanically etched over 12 hours.

261
00:16:54,020 --> 00:16:57,900
Once complete, the reduction hub must be painstakingly cleaned.

262
00:16:59,040 --> 00:17:00,640
You have to be careful.

263
00:17:01,280 --> 00:17:04,480
If you are a little bit too overzealous, you'll ruin it.

264
00:17:04,481 --> 00:17:08,720
So it's a very meticulously controlled bit of work

265
00:17:08,721 --> 00:17:11,400
where you are using little pieces of stone,

266
00:17:11,900 --> 00:17:14,520
sanding pads, things like that, to get rid of the lines.

267
00:17:15,180 --> 00:17:16,680
The tiniest stray lines,

268
00:17:16,880 --> 00:17:20,420
or even the slightest speck of dust, will ruin the dyes.

269
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:22,840
From this, which is a positive,

270
00:17:23,300 --> 00:17:25,940
they will be able to make a series of dyes, which are negatives,

271
00:17:26,100 --> 00:17:30,420
and the negatives will be used to actually stamp the coins

272
00:17:30,421 --> 00:17:32,220
that will wind up in your pocket.

273
00:17:32,221 --> 00:17:36,220
Once clean, the hub is placed in the die press.

274
00:17:36,221 --> 00:17:42,220
This machine centers the reduction hub over a cone-shaped cylinder.

275
00:17:42,221 --> 00:17:46,220
A press descends, flattening the hub and cylinder together.

276
00:17:46,221 --> 00:17:51,220
The etched image of Abraham Lincoln is forced onto the steel cylinder.

277
00:17:51,221 --> 00:17:55,220
What emerges from the die press is the master die,

278
00:17:55,221 --> 00:17:59,220
which has a recessed rather than raised image.

279
00:17:59,221 --> 00:18:05,220
After even more cleaning, the dye is ready for work on the production line, where it

280
00:18:05,221 --> 00:18:11,760
will stamp out millions of shiny new United States coins.

281
00:18:11,761 --> 00:18:16,840
Each one of those coins begins as a 1,500-foot-long metal coil.

282
00:18:16,841 --> 00:18:22,700
This coil is pure copper, sandwiched between layers of manganese brass.

283
00:18:22,701 --> 00:18:28,380
The coils are unrolled and fed through a blanking press, which punches out round disks, called

284
00:18:28,381 --> 00:18:29,183
blanks.

285
00:18:29,184 --> 00:18:38,700
The leftover metal, or webbing, gets melted down and recycled, while the new blanks

286
00:18:38,701 --> 00:18:44,005
head to an annealing furnace for a heat treatment.

287
00:18:44,006 --> 00:18:48,780
This alters the physical makeup of the metal,

288
00:18:48,781 --> 00:18:52,437
softening its surface in preparation for coining.

289
00:18:52,438 --> 00:18:55,720
After washing and drying, the blanks are run

290
00:18:55,721 --> 00:19:02,020
through an upsetting mill. This machine raises a rim around the edges. The mill

291
00:19:02,021 --> 00:19:06,820
consists of a rotating wheel with a groove on its edge. The grooved edge of

292
00:19:06,821 --> 00:19:12,180
the wheel is fitted to a curved section called a shoe which has a corresponding

293
00:19:12,181 --> 00:19:17,720
groove. The distance between the wheel and the shoe gets progressively narrower.

294
00:19:17,721 --> 00:19:24,400
As the blank is rolled along the groove, a raised rim is formed on both sides.

295
00:19:24,401 --> 00:19:27,400
Finally, the blanks are ready for striking.

296
00:19:27,401 --> 00:19:30,400
This happens in the coining press,

297
00:19:30,401 --> 00:19:35,400
where master dies stamp out 12 new coins every second.

298
00:19:35,401 --> 00:19:41,400
This intense striking process means the dies require constant replacing.

299
00:19:41,401 --> 00:19:45,400
They're under such pressure when they strike a coin.

300
00:19:45,401 --> 00:19:49,400
For example, to make a dollar coin, it takes about 80 tons of pressure.

301
00:19:49,401 --> 00:19:53,400
We make 12 coins per second.

302
00:19:53,401 --> 00:19:58,340
So those guys are hitting together using 80 tons of pressure 12 times a second.

303
00:20:06,180 --> 00:20:12,400
Every batch of new coins is carefully inspected for the slightest defect that could indicate a worn-out die.

304
00:20:12,660 --> 00:20:18,400
On the side of every coining press, there's a trap, and it takes about 10 minutes to fill that trap.

305
00:20:18,401 --> 00:20:23,960
And once it's filled, the operator's going to take the last two coins that were struck

306
00:20:23,961 --> 00:20:27,760
and are going to look at them to make sure that they're the best quality that can be made.

307
00:20:28,300 --> 00:20:32,900
Any imperfections means the entire batch of coins is sent to the recycling bin.

308
00:20:33,980 --> 00:20:36,820
Every coin has to be exactly alike.

309
00:20:36,960 --> 00:20:38,260
Every coin has to be perfect.

310
00:20:39,100 --> 00:20:42,940
If they're not, you're going to try and put them into a vending machine and they're not going to work.

311
00:20:43,520 --> 00:20:45,840
Plus, they're the coins of the United States.

312
00:20:46,040 --> 00:20:47,600
So we want them to be perfect.

313
00:20:47,601 --> 00:20:50,160
We're proud to be making a perfect product here.

314
00:20:56,820 --> 00:20:59,320
Perfect batches are sent to the counting machines,

315
00:20:59,940 --> 00:21:01,300
where they're bagged,

316
00:21:03,520 --> 00:21:04,220
weighed,

317
00:21:07,500 --> 00:21:09,140
and sent out into the world.

318
00:21:10,060 --> 00:21:12,640
These new coins will end up any number of places,

319
00:21:13,180 --> 00:21:14,820
but one of the most captivating

320
00:21:14,821 --> 00:21:19,420
just happens to be 164 feet below the Earth's surface

321
00:21:19,421 --> 00:21:22,560
in one of the most lucrative wishing wells on the planet.

322
00:21:27,160 --> 00:21:30,520
Throughout a coin's estimated 25-year lifespan,

323
00:21:31,060 --> 00:21:34,560
it's almost guaranteed to travel to some unexpected places.

324
00:21:35,940 --> 00:21:39,780
Like wishing wells, it's estimated that worldwide,

325
00:21:40,500 --> 00:21:42,920
people toss more than a million pounds of coins

326
00:21:42,921 --> 00:21:44,560
into wishing wells each year.

327
00:21:44,820 --> 00:21:47,820
Most of this gets donated to charity,

328
00:21:47,821 --> 00:21:50,820
which means the coins have to come out of the well.

329
00:21:50,821 --> 00:21:53,820
Not a terribly difficult task,

330
00:21:53,821 --> 00:21:59,820
unless the wishing well is 164 feet below the Earth's surface.

331
00:21:59,821 --> 00:22:03,820
Luray Caverns in Virginia's Shenandoah Valley

332
00:22:03,821 --> 00:22:07,820
is more than 400 million years old.

333
00:22:07,821 --> 00:22:11,820
It's the most popular cave system in the eastern U.S.

334
00:22:11,821 --> 00:22:18,820
And since 1954, one of the main attractions here has been this natural pool.

335
00:22:18,821 --> 00:22:24,820
It's one of the most lucrative wishing wells on the planet.

336
00:22:24,821 --> 00:22:27,820
One, two, three.

337
00:22:27,821 --> 00:22:29,820
Yes.

338
00:22:29,821 --> 00:22:34,820
We're talking about a lot of coins.

339
00:22:34,821 --> 00:22:43,820
Precisely since 1954, 36,072,131 coins,

340
00:22:43,821 --> 00:22:49,820
amounting to over 134 tons of money donated to charity.

341
00:22:49,821 --> 00:22:53,820
A grand total of almost a million dollars.

342
00:22:55,820 --> 00:22:58,820
Collecting all these coins is not an easy job.

343
00:22:58,821 --> 00:23:02,820
The well is six feet deep, the deepest pool in the caverns.

344
00:23:02,821 --> 00:23:04,820
in a water conference.

345
00:23:04,920 --> 00:23:07,920
Waiting in is not an option.

346
00:23:08,020 --> 00:23:11,820
Instead, the entire well has to be drained once a year

347
00:23:11,920 --> 00:23:14,860
by Luray's maintenance staff.

348
00:23:14,960 --> 00:23:19,060
We're getting ready to pump the water out of the wishing well.

349
00:23:19,160 --> 00:23:23,580
And we do this annually to collect all of the coins.

350
00:23:23,680 --> 00:23:27,380
OK, you get the hose.

351
00:23:27,480 --> 00:23:29,480
It's a two-day process, and it all

352
00:23:29,481 --> 00:23:33,480
by lowering a sump pump down into the pool.

353
00:23:33,481 --> 00:23:36,080
This is the same type of pump homeowners often use

354
00:23:36,081 --> 00:23:38,880
to remove water from flooded basements.

355
00:23:38,881 --> 00:23:42,480
OK, I'm going to plug the pump up.

356
00:23:42,481 --> 00:23:43,480
OK.

357
00:23:43,481 --> 00:23:44,480
There we go.

358
00:23:44,481 --> 00:23:46,780
The sump pump will take water out of the wishing well

359
00:23:46,781 --> 00:23:49,880
and store it in an adjacent pool.

360
00:23:49,881 --> 00:23:52,280
Later, once the coins are collected,

361
00:23:52,281 --> 00:23:54,880
workers will refill the well.

362
00:23:54,881 --> 00:23:58,280
The water in this pool, as in any of the pools

363
00:23:58,281 --> 00:24:02,680
in the caverns is seepage, and that is from water

364
00:24:02,681 --> 00:24:04,720
that just drips down from the surface

365
00:24:04,721 --> 00:24:07,000
over a long period of time.

366
00:24:07,001 --> 00:24:09,220
Now, if we were to leave this water out

367
00:24:09,221 --> 00:24:12,100
and let it seep back in, naturally,

368
00:24:12,101 --> 00:24:14,520
it would take a very long time.

369
00:24:14,521 --> 00:24:18,080
But by pumping this back, we may lose a little bit,

370
00:24:18,081 --> 00:24:21,160
but the seepage from the ground above

371
00:24:21,161 --> 00:24:25,100
will fill it back up to its normal height.

372
00:24:25,101 --> 00:24:27,620
Once the sump pump starts working,

373
00:24:27,621 --> 00:24:29,820
It will take eight hours to drain the pool.

374
00:24:37,120 --> 00:24:42,240
The next morning, a gold mine of treasure awaits Luray's maintenance staff.

375
00:24:45,080 --> 00:24:51,340
Workers armed with burlap sacks, shovels, and wheelbarrows survey the massive piles of coins.

376
00:24:51,341 --> 00:24:58,340
In some areas, the coins have accumulated in mounds more than two feet deep.

377
00:24:58,341 --> 00:25:07,340
Right now we're cleaning the coins down off the walls, putting them into the lowest point,

378
00:25:07,341 --> 00:25:12,340
and from there we'll shovel them into buckets, put the buckets of coins into bags,

379
00:25:12,341 --> 00:25:16,340
about 200 bags on average per year.

380
00:25:16,341 --> 00:25:25,500
There's an estimated $30,000 to $40,000 in this batch, which will all go to several different

381
00:25:25,501 --> 00:25:27,860
charities designated by the town of Leray.

382
00:25:29,700 --> 00:25:35,080
Within our small community in Leray, there is a secret committee that is assembled that

383
00:25:35,081 --> 00:25:38,580
determines the amounts and the recipients of these contributions.

384
00:25:46,340 --> 00:25:52,880
It takes the team about four hours to clear the pool,

385
00:25:54,180 --> 00:25:56,240
but the job is far from done.

386
00:25:56,920 --> 00:26:01,220
Now the crew has to get thousands of pounds of coins out of the cavern.

387
00:26:03,800 --> 00:26:06,280
The wishing well is about a half a mile from the exit,

388
00:26:06,740 --> 00:26:09,500
and with barrels that weigh about 200 pounds apiece,

389
00:26:10,200 --> 00:26:12,460
teamwork is definitely a big factor.

390
00:26:12,461 --> 00:26:17,460
It's a long, winding journey out of the cavern,

391
00:26:17,560 --> 00:26:20,460
and the crew begins a wheelbarrow relay.

392
00:26:32,520 --> 00:26:37,460
At the end of the path, there's one final obstacle.

393
00:26:37,560 --> 00:26:42,460
It's a workout worthy of the local high school football team.

394
00:26:42,461 --> 00:26:51,200
Every year, the boys volunteer their time to help haul sacks of coins up several flights of stairs.

395
00:26:52,740 --> 00:26:55,580
It's a good workout for a good cause.

396
00:26:56,380 --> 00:27:02,240
Over the years, some of the national health organizations that have received donations from the Luray Caverns Wishing Well

397
00:27:02,241 --> 00:27:09,420
are the American Cancer Society, the March of Dimes, the Juvenile Diabetes Research Foundation, among many others.

398
00:27:09,421 --> 00:27:15,040
between $30,000 and $40,000 is taken from this pool of water each year.

399
00:27:16,860 --> 00:27:19,980
But the coins have been sitting in water for a year.

400
00:27:22,180 --> 00:27:25,440
Many of them are corroded and covered with slime.

401
00:27:26,260 --> 00:27:30,240
In other words, this is dirty money that no bank will touch.

402
00:27:32,300 --> 00:27:37,220
But one enterprising company has figured out a way to turn that toxic treasure...

403
00:27:37,221 --> 00:27:38,540
Sweet penny! Treasure! Treasure!

404
00:27:38,541 --> 00:27:40,780
into cold, hard cash.

405
00:27:43,860 --> 00:27:48,300
What do you do with $30,000 worth of slimy coins

406
00:27:48,301 --> 00:27:50,560
that came from the bottom of a wishing well?

407
00:27:51,420 --> 00:27:52,540
Just ask this guy.

408
00:27:53,980 --> 00:27:55,920
Walt McKay of Dallas, Texas,

409
00:27:56,360 --> 00:27:59,240
has created a thriving business out of dirty money.

410
00:28:00,040 --> 00:28:02,100
Walt owns FountainCoins.com,

411
00:28:02,620 --> 00:28:04,620
a small company with a valuable mission.

412
00:28:05,320 --> 00:28:07,240
Turn those grimy fountain coins

413
00:28:07,241 --> 00:28:09,860
into something bankable.

414
00:28:12,140 --> 00:28:13,920
Well, when we get the coins,

415
00:28:14,700 --> 00:28:16,800
usually they've been sitting around for a while,

416
00:28:17,100 --> 00:28:18,360
and at the bottom of the bucket,

417
00:28:19,240 --> 00:28:21,220
the stuff that came out of the bottom of the fountain

418
00:28:21,221 --> 00:28:24,040
or the water feature can be pretty grimy.

419
00:28:24,480 --> 00:28:27,180
By the time we dump it out and get a nice whiff of it,

420
00:28:27,260 --> 00:28:28,140
it's pretty ripe.

421
00:28:29,800 --> 00:28:32,960
Walt has been cleaning fountain coins since 2003.

422
00:28:34,080 --> 00:28:35,900
It may sound like an easy task,

423
00:28:35,901 --> 00:28:39,700
But coming up with a workable method was a lesson in trial and error.

424
00:28:40,500 --> 00:28:46,020
I broke our clothes washer by putting coins in a bag and tumbling around in there.

425
00:28:46,100 --> 00:28:47,200
I had to repair that myself.

426
00:28:48,180 --> 00:28:51,680
I broke our dishwasher pretty much the exact same way.

427
00:28:52,380 --> 00:28:56,440
Today, Walt uses a small cement mixer and an assortment of chemicals.

428
00:28:57,140 --> 00:29:00,620
Not a very high-tech process, but it gets the job done.

429
00:29:00,621 --> 00:29:10,220
First thing we do is dump all these dirty coins in a cement mixer, and we rinse them

430
00:29:10,221 --> 00:29:16,380
several times so we get the floating debris and the majority of the dirt off of it.

431
00:29:16,381 --> 00:29:19,260
But dirt isn't the only thing Walt finds.

432
00:29:19,261 --> 00:29:23,740
It's just a world of treasure in here.

433
00:29:23,741 --> 00:29:30,500
There's my lunchtime toothpick, marbles, broken jewelry pieces, a little bit of caulking

434
00:29:30,501 --> 00:29:31,600
from the fountain there.

435
00:29:33,540 --> 00:29:34,240
Ooh.

436
00:29:36,040 --> 00:29:37,560
That would be juicy fruit.

437
00:29:46,740 --> 00:29:48,840
You can see how all the stuff that floats

438
00:29:48,841 --> 00:29:50,360
is already starting to come off.

439
00:29:50,860 --> 00:29:53,540
It's a nice, sweet, soupy mix

440
00:29:53,541 --> 00:29:55,720
of the stuff we're trying to get out.

441
00:29:56,140 --> 00:29:57,420
After the initial rinse,

442
00:29:57,760 --> 00:30:00,040
Walt has to get rid of some of the organic material.

443
00:30:00,500 --> 00:30:03,200
slime, algae, or moss that collects on the coins.

444
00:30:03,360 --> 00:30:08,600
And just a splash of this chlorine is all it takes.

445
00:30:12,480 --> 00:30:15,080
We'll let it work its magic for a little while.

446
00:30:16,960 --> 00:30:18,680
Oh, the water's already turning lighter.

447
00:30:19,280 --> 00:30:20,200
Take a look at it.

448
00:30:22,200 --> 00:30:22,900
Good.

449
00:30:23,660 --> 00:30:27,400
This water's just like water that would be in a swimming pool at this point.

450
00:30:27,401 --> 00:30:30,600
Next, a dash of laundry detergent.

451
00:30:31,160 --> 00:30:33,660
This will cut through any remaining grease and grime.

452
00:30:35,580 --> 00:30:38,380
Looks like my hot tub at home after a big party.

453
00:30:41,320 --> 00:30:46,140
After a few minutes, the coins are ready for the next addition, an abrasive.

454
00:30:46,840 --> 00:30:55,660
The reason we need the abrasive is some of these have so much scale or calcification

455
00:30:55,661 --> 00:30:59,140
that they won't even fit through a commercial coin counter.

456
00:31:00,020 --> 00:31:03,260
Our mission here is to make this penny worth a penny.

457
00:31:07,580 --> 00:31:12,540
This is our own secret sauce from Texas Hook'em.

458
00:31:15,760 --> 00:31:19,280
It'll take about two hours for the abrasive to do its job.

459
00:31:22,020 --> 00:31:25,540
But when it's finished, these coins look a lot different.

460
00:31:25,660 --> 00:31:28,960
You can see how much cleaner the coins are at this point.

461
00:31:29,300 --> 00:31:32,900
There's no scale, and there's nothing to stop it

462
00:31:32,901 --> 00:31:35,100
from going through the chute at the bank.

463
00:31:36,400 --> 00:31:38,380
An acid wash shines them up

464
00:31:38,381 --> 00:31:40,200
and gets rid of any lingering debris.

465
00:31:41,840 --> 00:31:45,120
Then the batch is dried and spread out to be sorted.

466
00:31:47,760 --> 00:31:49,160
Dangerous items are tossed.

467
00:31:50,020 --> 00:31:51,200
This is the torture.

468
00:31:51,920 --> 00:31:53,280
Luckily, it didn't stick me,

469
00:31:53,281 --> 00:31:55,940
but it goes into the torture box here.

470
00:31:56,920 --> 00:31:58,360
Foreign coins are saved.

471
00:31:58,480 --> 00:32:01,180
Oh, good. Okay, we got a euro, five euro cent.

472
00:32:01,740 --> 00:32:02,800
What else do we have?

473
00:32:03,280 --> 00:32:04,220
And good old Mexico.

474
00:32:04,860 --> 00:32:07,100
Texas is close to Mexico, so I have a lot of pesos.

475
00:32:08,100 --> 00:32:11,700
And every so often, Walt finds something really exciting.

476
00:32:12,060 --> 00:32:13,460
Wheat penny, treasure, treasure.

477
00:32:13,920 --> 00:32:14,660
Punch the button.

478
00:32:15,540 --> 00:32:16,480
There we go.

479
00:32:16,940 --> 00:32:17,640
All right.

480
00:32:17,960 --> 00:32:20,660
It's fun when we find old coins, the wheat pennies.

481
00:32:20,661 --> 00:32:26,680
Wheaties were the regular U.S. penny between 1909 and 1958.

482
00:32:27,440 --> 00:32:30,680
They're popular among modern collectors because they're old,

483
00:32:30,960 --> 00:32:35,820
but also because they're almost entirely copper, unlike new pennies.

484
00:32:36,240 --> 00:32:42,320
The new pennies, for many years, have been zinc with a copper plating,

485
00:32:42,760 --> 00:32:48,240
and the copper plating is relatively thin, and it gets eaten through,

486
00:32:48,241 --> 00:32:52,920
So we have to pick out all these pennies, and the ones we try to make, you know,

487
00:32:53,000 --> 00:32:55,120
it's still, you're only talking about one cent at a time.

488
00:32:55,240 --> 00:32:57,120
It's the hardest part of the job.

489
00:32:58,020 --> 00:33:02,900
A penny won't buy much, but the biggest problem with it is this surprising fact.

490
00:33:03,300 --> 00:33:05,700
It costs more than a cent to make a cent.

491
00:33:07,200 --> 00:33:09,880
Zinc and copper have skyrocketed in price.

492
00:33:10,260 --> 00:33:13,220
It does cost more than a penny to make a penny these days.

493
00:33:13,221 --> 00:33:16,460
It depends upon the metal market of the month

494
00:33:16,461 --> 00:33:19,040
and how much we can purchase the metal for each month.

495
00:33:19,420 --> 00:33:24,100
But by and large, it's a cent and a half to 1.7 cents right now,

496
00:33:24,160 --> 00:33:27,160
that's public information, of how much it costs us to make a penny.

497
00:33:28,220 --> 00:33:31,000
The economy of that may not make much sense,

498
00:33:31,560 --> 00:33:34,640
but the penny is the most widely used coin in the U.S.

499
00:33:35,140 --> 00:33:39,100
Us Americans are very emotionally attached to our penny,

500
00:33:39,520 --> 00:33:40,960
and there's two sides of the argument.

501
00:33:40,961 --> 00:33:44,140
that if we should continue the penny, we shouldn't continue the penny.

502
00:33:44,980 --> 00:33:50,520
At least for now, most people, as well as Congress, want the penny to stay around.

503
00:33:51,080 --> 00:33:55,120
And that's probably a good thing, because even cents add up.

504
00:33:55,660 --> 00:33:58,200
We want to count every single penny that we can,

505
00:33:58,320 --> 00:34:01,480
because all the proceeds of these coins go to charities.

506
00:34:02,640 --> 00:34:05,120
After sorting through the entire batch of coins,

507
00:34:05,460 --> 00:34:08,060
Walt bundles them up and puts them through a counting machine.

508
00:34:08,360 --> 00:34:10,160
Makes a neat sound. I love the sound.

509
00:34:10,960 --> 00:34:13,260
Every little click is another wish.

510
00:34:14,760 --> 00:34:18,580
We put about $50 worth into a bag like this.

511
00:34:18,640 --> 00:34:19,880
It weighs 31 pounds.

512
00:34:20,700 --> 00:34:23,740
I'd like to keep track of the weight of the coins,

513
00:34:24,120 --> 00:34:27,940
and I can tell how much debris and trash we've removed

514
00:34:27,941 --> 00:34:30,120
from the original gross weight.

515
00:34:30,860 --> 00:34:32,040
I have a net weight.

516
00:34:33,160 --> 00:34:35,660
The coins will be sent to the bank for a recount,

517
00:34:36,300 --> 00:34:38,280
and Walt will take a small cut for his troubles.

518
00:34:38,281 --> 00:34:41,680
A check for the rest is sent to the customer.

519
00:34:41,681 --> 00:34:46,460
I have a form that I send every customer.

520
00:34:46,461 --> 00:34:53,500
It gives a precise count of every penny, every nickel, dime, quarter, whatever.

521
00:34:53,501 --> 00:34:59,000
I take the time to look through because that's what I like to do.

522
00:34:59,001 --> 00:35:03,860
These coins will go back out into the world, where many of them will undoubtedly find their

523
00:35:03,861 --> 00:35:07,520
way into a coin-operative machine.

524
00:35:07,521 --> 00:35:09,840
comes out is anybody's guess.

525
00:35:11,620 --> 00:35:15,560
The coin-operated industry provides some useful services.

526
00:35:17,160 --> 00:35:18,600
Take the car wash.

527
00:35:19,120 --> 00:35:22,540
It's the most popular and convenient way to wash our cars.

528
00:35:22,980 --> 00:35:28,400
So it was just a matter of time before someone decided to shrink it down to doggy size.

529
00:35:28,860 --> 00:35:33,220
Here in Hoboken, New Jersey, coin-operated has a new leash on life.

530
00:35:33,221 --> 00:35:35,180
This is the K9000,

531
00:35:35,181 --> 00:35:38,720
a coin-operated shower for our four-legged friends.

532
00:35:38,721 --> 00:35:42,920
On the weekends, we could do up to $60 to $70.

533
00:35:43,520 --> 00:35:44,300
Rufus, come.

534
00:35:47,340 --> 00:35:50,820
Just insert money, and with a simple flip of a switch,

535
00:35:51,260 --> 00:35:54,780
you can shampoo, rinse, even condition.

536
00:35:55,820 --> 00:35:59,460
Hidden inside the machine are all natural organic products

537
00:35:59,461 --> 00:36:01,720
and a water heater that allows customers

538
00:36:01,721 --> 00:36:03,200
to choose their own temperature.

539
00:36:03,380 --> 00:36:04,260
Say hello to America.

540
00:36:05,180 --> 00:36:12,180
A flea and tick rinse is the final coat, and a blow dry tops it all off.

541
00:36:12,181 --> 00:36:16,180
$15 for small dogs, $20 for large ones.

542
00:36:16,181 --> 00:36:23,180
And whether you have four legs or two, the K9000 takes convenience to a whole new level.

543
00:36:23,181 --> 00:36:30,180
The phrase I get the most is, I'll never ever wash my dog at home ever again.

544
00:36:30,181 --> 00:36:34,180
Efficient self-service is the cornerstone of the coin-operative business.

545
00:36:34,181 --> 00:36:40,520
And when it comes to vending, self-service is a $300 billion industry.

546
00:36:41,260 --> 00:36:48,260
Every day, 100 million Americans insert money into one of 7 million vending machines scattered across the country.

547
00:36:49,020 --> 00:36:54,140
We buy everything, from sodas and snacks to aspirin and ice cream.

548
00:36:55,300 --> 00:36:58,860
But this machine pushes the limits of traditional vending.

549
00:36:58,861 --> 00:37:05,860
It's called the 24-hour bait shop, and it caters to fishermen, who tend to keep odd hours.

550
00:37:05,861 --> 00:37:10,860
People love them because they can get their products 24-7.

551
00:37:10,861 --> 00:37:13,860
The bait shops don't have to be open.

552
00:37:13,861 --> 00:37:18,860
You can go there at 4 o'clock in the morning, 10 o'clock at night, any time of day or night,

553
00:37:18,861 --> 00:37:21,860
get a full wide array of bait and tackle.

554
00:37:21,861 --> 00:37:27,860
The temperature-controlled machine is stocked with creatures only an outdoorsman could love.

555
00:37:27,861 --> 00:37:35,320
Minnows, worms, even crayfish, all perfectly alive in their own biodegradable containers.

556
00:37:36,180 --> 00:37:42,700
It's just not your typical bag of potato chips or your bottle of soda or can of soda.

557
00:37:42,880 --> 00:37:47,560
We have all different size packaging, a wide array of things that have to go in there.

558
00:37:48,240 --> 00:37:51,940
The only thing we can't vend, we haven't figured out how to vend, is a rod.

559
00:37:52,600 --> 00:37:56,680
The 24-hour bait shop takes vending to a new level of sophistication.

560
00:37:56,681 --> 00:38:05,880
Just like this machine, the Wine Kiosk, is a massive high-tech vending machine that recently hit the scene in Pennsylvania,

561
00:38:07,000 --> 00:38:10,500
where by law, wine is only sold in state-owned stores.

562
00:38:11,920 --> 00:38:18,720
What this kiosk is going to do is bring the convenience of purchasing wine and spirits to customers who are shopping within this grocery store.

563
00:38:19,020 --> 00:38:23,500
So essentially, we've just brought that to them, as opposed to having them go out to purchase the wine and spirits.

564
00:38:23,501 --> 00:38:25,700
The process is simple.

565
00:38:26,380 --> 00:38:28,460
Just choose a bottle on the machine's touchscreen,

566
00:38:29,100 --> 00:38:30,840
insert a driver's license,

567
00:38:31,180 --> 00:38:33,360
and smile for the camera.

568
00:38:33,740 --> 00:38:35,060
On the other side of this camera,

569
00:38:35,380 --> 00:38:37,800
there's actually a live individual who watches

570
00:38:37,801 --> 00:38:40,860
and documents every single transaction that has occurred.

571
00:38:41,600 --> 00:38:43,860
A state employee remotely approves the sale

572
00:38:43,861 --> 00:38:45,640
after verifying all information

573
00:38:45,641 --> 00:38:48,220
and ensuring the buyer isn't drunk.

574
00:38:48,600 --> 00:38:49,980
Please take a deep breath

575
00:38:49,981 --> 00:38:52,920
and blow firmly into the breath alcohol sensor

576
00:38:52,921 --> 00:38:54,460
as shown in the video.

577
00:38:56,220 --> 00:38:57,460
Once a payment is made,

578
00:38:57,860 --> 00:38:59,340
the kiosk locates the wine

579
00:38:59,341 --> 00:39:01,280
and allows the buyer to take their bottle.

580
00:39:01,360 --> 00:39:02,660
The bottles are in the cabinets

581
00:39:02,661 --> 00:39:04,540
indicated by the fashion image.

582
00:39:04,940 --> 00:39:06,060
Essentially what we've tried to do

583
00:39:06,061 --> 00:39:07,800
is keep the experience of buying wine

584
00:39:07,801 --> 00:39:09,100
that customers are used to.

585
00:39:09,440 --> 00:39:11,920
So they're not looking at a vending purchase

586
00:39:11,921 --> 00:39:13,540
so that when they walk up to the wine

587
00:39:13,541 --> 00:39:14,680
they can browse the wine,

588
00:39:14,820 --> 00:39:16,660
they can look and see which wine they want.

589
00:39:16,980 --> 00:39:18,840
They can also, when they purchase the wine,

590
00:39:18,920 --> 00:39:20,300
they're actually opening the door

591
00:39:20,301 --> 00:39:21,920
and taking the bottle out,

592
00:39:21,921 --> 00:39:23,020
just like they would in a store.

593
00:39:23,400 --> 00:39:25,140
So essentially we've kept the experience,

594
00:39:25,320 --> 00:39:27,260
but have allowed them to do it in a self-service manner.

595
00:39:27,980 --> 00:39:29,360
Thank you for your purchase.

596
00:39:29,860 --> 00:39:32,980
The wine kiosk uses the latest in vending technology.

597
00:39:34,820 --> 00:39:37,320
A sure sign that the coin-operated industry

598
00:39:37,321 --> 00:39:40,640
is quickly evolving into a highly mechanized business

599
00:39:40,641 --> 00:39:44,620
that sells products too pricey for just a few coins.

600
00:39:46,400 --> 00:39:47,720
But for one company,

601
00:39:48,300 --> 00:39:50,780
a few coins has been a successful business model

602
00:39:50,781 --> 00:39:51,760
for half a century.

603
00:39:51,920 --> 00:39:55,920
Seacoast Manufacturing in Fairhope, Alabama,

604
00:39:55,921 --> 00:39:59,920
designs and manufactures classic coin-operated viewers

605
00:39:59,921 --> 00:40:04,920
that have given us up-close looks at iconic places since 1960.

606
00:40:04,921 --> 00:40:08,920
You put your money in, you wind it up, and you have a view.

607
00:40:08,921 --> 00:40:11,920
You don't vend anything.

608
00:40:11,921 --> 00:40:16,920
You're vending a view of something that's already there.

609
00:40:16,921 --> 00:40:18,920
It's a simple concept,

610
00:40:18,921 --> 00:40:21,920
But these telescopes and binoculars are tough,

611
00:40:22,020 --> 00:40:25,920
built to withstand almost anything, even hurricanes.

612
00:40:26,020 --> 00:40:28,920
I remember a time that one had been underwater

613
00:40:29,020 --> 00:40:31,920
after a hurricane for two weeks.

614
00:40:32,020 --> 00:40:35,920
And in a matter of two hours, Dad and I cleaned it up,

615
00:40:36,020 --> 00:40:37,920
and it was back working.

616
00:40:38,020 --> 00:40:40,920
The secret to this incredible durability?

617
00:40:41,020 --> 00:40:44,920
Stainless steel, aluminum, and brass bodies.

618
00:40:45,020 --> 00:40:47,920
And a complete lack of electronics.

619
00:40:47,921 --> 00:40:51,920
No batteries, no wires, no power adapters.

620
00:40:51,921 --> 00:40:55,920
Just a simple mechanism that works like a wind-up clock.

621
00:40:55,921 --> 00:40:59,920
When coins are inserted, they trigger a timing mechanism

622
00:40:59,921 --> 00:41:03,920
that flips open a shutter. The timing mechanism

623
00:41:03,921 --> 00:41:07,920
holds the shutter open for a minute and a half, and then flips it back

624
00:41:07,921 --> 00:41:11,920
over the eyepiece.

625
00:41:11,921 --> 00:41:15,920
What's great about these machines is that they are made to

626
00:41:15,921 --> 00:41:21,920
to extend the elements, you know, extreme heat, extreme cold, very little maintenance.

627
00:41:21,921 --> 00:41:29,920
These machines are built to last, using time-tested techniques that have changed very little in half a century.

628
00:41:33,920 --> 00:41:41,920
The only change since 1960 is that you had to put a dime in it and now you have to put 50 cents in it.

629
00:41:41,921 --> 00:41:44,800
The only change is change.

630
00:41:48,260 --> 00:41:50,060
Today, for just two quarters,

631
00:41:50,680 --> 00:41:54,180
anyone can enjoy an up-close view of the natural world

632
00:41:54,181 --> 00:41:56,260
or the man-made one.

633
00:41:57,280 --> 00:41:59,820
It's a coin-operated beast for the eyes

634
00:41:59,821 --> 00:42:02,160
until your minute and a half is up.

635
00:42:02,900 --> 00:42:04,300
And when that happens,

636
00:42:04,680 --> 00:42:08,000
there will always be somewhere else to drop those extra coins.

637
00:42:08,001 --> 00:42:14,620
because even in a world that's becoming less dependent on small change,

638
00:42:15,400 --> 00:42:20,060
those pennies, dimes, nickels, quarters, and Lincoln dollars

639
00:42:20,061 --> 00:42:22,620
can always go into a piggy bank.

640
00:42:23,220 --> 00:42:24,440
That's just good sense.

